WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.230
Leo Mordasini: Hey John, how are you doing this week?

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:12.100
John Smith: Pretty good, a bit stretched across two projects but managing.

3
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:20.000
Leo Mordasini: Yeah, let's talk about that. Can you have the SAP 2.0 deck ready by Thursday?

4
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:30.750
John Smith: Thursday is tight. I'm still waiting on the integration test results from the platform team before I can finalise the numbers slide.

5
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:38.200
Leo Mordasini: Okay, I'll chase platform this afternoon. If they can get you the results by tomorrow EOD that still gives you Wednesday to pull it together.

6
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:44.300
John Smith: That would work. I'd also really appreciate you sending over the Q2 roadmap so I can make sure the deck aligns with the broader narrative.

7
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:50.100
Leo Mordasini: Absolutely — I'll send it to you by Thursday morning. I should have sent it sooner, that's on me.

8
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:58.800
John Smith: No worries. On SAP 2.0 more broadly — the architecture review landed well last week. The client seemed genuinely impressed with the phased migration approach.

9
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:07.400
Leo Mordasini: I heard! And I wanted to call that out specifically — you led the demo completely solo. That was a big moment and you nailed it.

10
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:14.500
John Smith: Thanks, I appreciate that. Honestly it gave me a real confidence boost. Prepping for it on my own made the delivery feel a lot more natural.

11
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:22.300
Leo Mordasini: It showed. Okay, switching gears — you mentioned being stretched. How many active workstreams are you juggling right now?

12
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:33.600
John Smith: Three if you count the SAP 2.0 prep, the legacy data migration tickets, and the on-call rotation that kicked in this week. The on-call is the unpredictable one.

13
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:42.800
Leo Mordasini: That's a lot. I want to make sure it's sustainable. Are you able to get proper breaks, or does it feel like you're context-switching all day?

14
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:53.900
John Smith: Mostly context-switching. I've been blocking out mornings for deep work but the afternoon Slack volume makes it hard to protect. I'm not burning out or anything, just... a bit flat by end of day.

15
00:01:54.300 --> 00:02:02.100
Leo Mordasini: I hear you. "Flat by end of day" is worth paying attention to. What would help most — reducing interruptions, dropping something from your plate, or something else?

16
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:11.700
John Smith: Probably a combination. If we could hand the legacy migration tickets to Priya for the next two sprints that would free up a real chunk. She mentioned wanting more backend exposure anyway.

17
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:19.400
Leo Mordasini: That's a good idea — I'll align with Priya's manager tomorrow and make it official. Consider that done. Anything else weighing on you?

18
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:28.600
John Smith: Not work-wise. I did want to say — I feel like our one-on-ones have been more useful lately. The structured check-ins help me stay on top of what actually matters versus just reacting.

19
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:37.200
Leo Mordasini: Really glad to hear that. I've been trying to come in more prepared. Let's keep this format going. Same time next week?

20
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.000
John Smith: Sounds good. Thanks Leo.
